Effective parameter estimation in Bayesian analysis often relies on Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods; however, their performance is critically sensitive to the variance of the posterior, which can reach extreme levels in complex models. Recent research indicates that auxiliary MCMC techniques can significantly reduce this variance and improve sample size efficiency relative to standard methods. We demonstrate scenarios where variance suppression is so profound that auxiliary methods outperform standard MCMC by orders of magnitude and, in certain cases, even surpass non-MCMC approaches such as standard importance sampling. These findings are substantiated through applications to linear regression with heterogeneous error structures and stochastic volatility modeling.

